WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.694
Respiratory drugs are split into three parts, and this first part covers the airway drugs.

2
00:00:07.694 --> 00:00:14.329
For each drug the study guide wants its class, its site of action, and its hazards.

3
00:00:14.329 --> 00:00:21.124
We cover acetylcysteine, beclomethasone, albuterol and levalbuterol, and ipratropium.

4
00:00:21.124 --> 00:00:33.115
Then the difference between short-acting and long-acting beta agonists, the leukotriene inhibitors, and the immunoglobulins and monoclonal antibodies.

5
00:00:33.115 --> 00:00:38.950
We finish with why albuterol shows up in the treatment of high potassium.

6
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:46.926
Acetylcysteine, Mucomyst, is a mucolytic: it reduces the thickness of mucus.

7
00:00:46.926 --> 00:00:50.292
Its site of action is the mucus itself.

8
00:00:50.292 --> 00:00:55.729
Mucus contains disulfide bonds, and acetylcysteine breaks them.

9
00:00:55.729 --> 00:01:05.741
It comes as ten and twenty percent, and the twenty percent is often diluted with saline, essentially to ten percent.

10
00:01:05.741 --> 00:01:20.327
Its hazards are bronchospasm, especially in asthmatics, so give a bronchodilator first; nausea or vomiting; a rotten egg smell; and mechanical obstruction of the airway.

11
00:01:20.327 --> 00:01:23.434
Do not mix it with some antibiotics.

12
00:01:23.434 --> 00:01:24.556
Does it work?

13
00:01:24.556 --> 00:01:37.416
The deck says no data show it is effective for any lung disease, and it should not be used as a mucoactive drug, and Hess calls its benefit unproven.

14
00:01:37.416 --> 00:01:47.342
Its most important use today, which the deck flags as very testable, is as the antidote for acetaminophen overdose.

15
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:54.351
Beclomethasone dipropionate, QVAR, is an inhaled corticosteroid.

16
00:01:54.351 --> 00:02:00.546
It is a controller, not a rescue drug, and not for acute severe asthma.

17
00:02:00.546 --> 00:02:07.962
It is used for maintenance control of chronic asthma, and for COPD with other agents.

18
00:02:07.962 --> 00:02:12.237
Hess notes it is a prodrug activated in the lung.

19
00:02:12.237 --> 00:02:25.412
Glucocorticoids are lipid soluble and act on receptors inside the cell, inducing anti-inflammatory proteins and inhibiting the enzyme phospholipase A2.

20
00:02:25.412 --> 00:02:35.708
Steroids also restore responsiveness to beta stimulation, by increasing the number and availability of beta receptors.

21
00:02:35.708 --> 00:02:44.521
The most common side effects are thrush and hoarseness, and less often cough and bronchoconstriction.

22
00:02:44.521 --> 00:02:50.454
Prevent them with the minimal dose, a spacer, and rinsing the mouth.

23
00:02:50.454 --> 00:02:59.092
Hess says inhaled steroids work once or twice a day, and Heuer lists QVAR at two puffs twice daily.

24
00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:11.868
Albuterol and levalbuterol are short-acting beta two agonists, rescue drugs, or relievers, for acute reversible airflow obstruction.

25
00:03:11.868 --> 00:03:20.652
Binding the beta receptor raises cyclic AMP, and beta two stimulation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle.

26
00:03:20.652 --> 00:03:29.436
The deck's class slide gives an onset of fifteen minutes and a duration of four to six hours for both.

27
00:03:29.436 --> 00:03:34.345
Albuterol is a fifty fifty mix of two mirror-image forms.

28
00:03:34.345 --> 00:03:42.527
The R form causes bronchodilation, and the S form is much less potent and may add side effects.

29
00:03:42.527 --> 00:03:49.330
Its nebulizer dose is two point five milligrams in three milliliters of saline.

30
00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:58.803
Levalbuterol, Xopenex, is the pure R form, and Hess says it is likely twice as potent, allowing half the dose.

31
00:03:58.803 --> 00:04:13.271
The beta agonist side effects are tremor, a faster heart rate and palpitations, tolerance, a fall in PaO2, higher glucose, lower potassium and paradoxical bronchospasm.

32
00:04:13.271 --> 00:04:21.625
Levalbuterol causes less tremor and heart rate change, but Hess says its superiority is unproven.

33
00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:31.781
Ipratropium bromide, Atrovent, is an anticholinergic bronchodilator, a muscarinic receptor antagonist.

34
00:04:31.781 --> 00:04:39.585
Parasympathetic nerves set the airway's resting tone, and anticholinergics block that tone.

35
00:04:39.585 --> 00:04:53.477
The M3 receptor on airway smooth muscle and glands causes bronchoconstriction, and Hess says ipratropium blocks muscarinic receptors without preferring a subtype.

36
00:04:53.477 --> 00:05:03.254
Because it is a quaternary relative of atropine, it does not cross membranes well, so it stays mostly in the lung.

37
00:05:03.254 --> 00:05:15.689
It is used for maintenance treatment of COPD, and sometimes in asthma, and with albuterol, as in Combivent and DuoNeb, the effect is synergistic.

38
00:05:15.689 --> 00:05:21.006
Its onset is several minutes, with a peak at one to two hours.

39
00:05:21.006 --> 00:05:28.981
The most common side effect is dry mouth, then cough and dilated pupils, so protect the eyes.

40
00:05:28.981 --> 00:05:40.558
The deck's doses are seventeen micrograms per puff, two puffs four times a day, or point five milligrams by nebulizer four times a day.

41
00:05:41.967 --> 00:05:55.293
Short-acting beta agonists are rescue drugs for acute obstruction: albuterol and levalbuterol, with an onset of fifteen minutes and a duration of four to six hours.

42
00:05:55.293 --> 00:06:17.801
Long-acting beta agonists are controllers, for maintenance bronchodilation and nighttime symptoms: salmeterol, formoterol and arformoterol, and the ultra-long indacaterol, olodaterol and vilanterol, with an onset of twenty minutes and a duration of twelve to twenty four hours.

43
00:06:17.801 --> 00:06:23.570
Short-acting agents are water soluble and long-acting ones fat soluble.

44
00:06:23.570 --> 00:06:33.159
Hess gives albuterol as needed, every four to six hours, salmeterol twice a day, and the ultra-long agents once a day.

45
00:06:33.159 --> 00:06:39.497
The deck gives formoterol three minutes to effect, lasting up to twelve hours.

46
00:06:39.497 --> 00:06:45.266
And the rule: long-acting beta agonists are never used alone in asthma.

47
00:06:45.266 --> 00:06:53.067
They need an inhaled steroid controller, and they do not replace short-acting agents for rescue.

48
00:06:54.467 --> 00:06:56.250
Pause and sort these.

49
00:06:56.250 --> 00:07:06.693
During an acute asthma attack, which of these can be used for rescue: albuterol, salmeterol, beclomethasone or montelukast?

50
00:07:06.693 --> 00:07:12.892
And what must a long-acting beta agonist always be paired with in asthma?

51
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.800
[6-second practice pause]

52
00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:25.971
Only albuterol, a short-acting beta agonist, is a rescue drug.

53
00:07:25.971 --> 00:07:31.368
Salmeterol is a long-acting controller with a slower onset.

54
00:07:31.368 --> 00:07:38.685
Beclomethasone is an inhaled steroid, a controller, not for acute severe asthma.

55
00:07:38.685 --> 00:07:44.540
Montelukast is a prophylactic controller, not for acute attacks.

56
00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:55.150
And a long-acting beta agonist must always be paired with an inhaled steroid controller in asthma, never used alone.

57
00:07:56.567 --> 00:08:06.657
Leukotrienes cause bronchoconstriction, mucus production and airway inflammation, and antileukotrienes block them in two ways.

58
00:08:06.657 --> 00:08:18.830
Montelukast, Singulair, the most tested, blocks the receptor, binding the leukotriene receptor with high affinity, and its side effects are mostly mild.

59
00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:23.074
Zafirlukast, Accolate, is also a receptor antagonist.

60
00:08:23.074 --> 00:08:38.050
It causes headache and stomach upset, Hess says to take it on an empty stomach, because food lowers its absorption by about forty percent, and it interacts with warfarin and theophylline.

61
00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:45.017
Zileuton, Zyflo, blocks production instead, by inhibiting the five-lipoxygenase enzyme.

62
00:08:45.017 --> 00:08:51.184
Monitor liver function, and it also interacts with theophylline and warfarin.

63
00:08:51.184 --> 00:08:56.149
All three are prophylactic controllers, not for acute attacks.

64
00:08:56.149 --> 00:09:08.242
They protect against exercise, aspirin and allergen triggered asthma, are not ideal alone, and work in only about fifty to seventy percent of patients.

65
00:09:09.667 --> 00:09:16.565
Immunoglobulins, or antibodies, are made by B lymphocytes, in five classes.

66
00:09:16.565 --> 00:09:24.474
IgE is the asthma antibody: it attaches to mast cells and triggers allergic reactions.

67
00:09:24.474 --> 00:09:36.523
IgA protects mucosal surfaces, IgG is the most common and fights infection, IgM is the first responder, and IgD is the fifth class.

68
00:09:36.523 --> 00:09:44.157
Monoclonal antibodies, the biologics, treat severe asthma by injection or infusion.

69
00:09:44.157 --> 00:09:56.113
They are add-ons, not for acute relief and not a replacement for inhaled steroids, and the most severe side effect is anaphylaxis.

70
00:09:56.113 --> 00:10:04.759
Omalizumab, Xolair, binds IgE and blocks it from attaching to mast cells, for allergic asthma.

71
00:10:04.759 --> 00:10:14.968
Benralizumab, mepolizumab and reslizumab block interleukin five, lowering eosinophils, for eosinophilic asthma.

72
00:10:14.968 --> 00:10:19.934
Palivizumab, Synagis, prevents RSV in at-risk infants.

73
00:10:19.934 --> 00:10:29.592
The decks call these antibodies both humanized IgE and IgG, and Hess calls omalizumab IgG, so answer IgG.

74
00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:35.884
Palivizumab works differently from the asthma biologics.

75
00:10:35.884 --> 00:10:41.117
It inhibits the F protein and prevents RSV from replicating.

76
00:10:41.117 --> 00:10:54.024
It is given to prevent RSV in infants with bronchopulmonary dysplasia, those born before thirty five weeks, and those with congenital heart disease.

77
00:10:54.024 --> 00:11:09.025
One gap remains: no source covers RSV immune globulin, and intravenous immune globulin appears only for neuromuscular disease, such as Guillain-Barré and myasthenia gravis.

78
00:11:10.433 --> 00:11:17.802
Both low and high potassium are reversible causes of cardiac arrest, among the five Hs.

79
00:11:17.802 --> 00:11:26.526
About ninety eight percent of body potassium is inside cells, where the sodium potassium pump keeps it.

80
00:11:26.526 --> 00:11:41.094
Albuterol stimulates beta two receptors on muscle and liver cells, which raises cyclic AMP and speeds the pump, so potassium moves into the cells and the serum level falls.

81
00:11:41.094 --> 00:11:46.092
It works within thirty minutes and lasts two to four hours.

82
00:11:46.092 --> 00:12:01.338
The dose for hyperkalemia is ten to twenty milligrams nebulized, far above the bronchodilator dose, and it can lower serum potassium by about half to one milliequivalent per liter.

83
00:12:01.338 --> 00:12:10.316
It is used with calcium gluconate, insulin with dextrose, bicarbonate if acidotic, and definitive removal.

84
00:12:10.316 --> 00:12:22.258
The exam anchor: albuterol does not remove potassium, it hides it inside the cells, so it is a bridge, not a cure, and potassium can rebound.

85
00:12:23.667 --> 00:12:24.491
One more.

86
00:12:24.491 --> 00:12:33.096
A patient with dangerous hyperkalemia gets nebulized albuterol, and the serum potassium falls.

87
00:12:33.096 --> 00:12:36.849
Has potassium been removed from the body?

88
00:12:36.849 --> 00:12:42.067
Why might it rise again, and roughly what dose was given?

89
00:12:42.967 --> 00:12:48.967
[6-second practice pause]

90
00:12:49.467 --> 00:12:50.067
No.

91
00:12:50.067 --> 00:12:57.336
Albuterol moves potassium into the cells, so total body potassium is unchanged.

92
00:12:57.336 --> 00:13:05.803
The effect lasts two to four hours, and then potassium may leak back out, so it can rebound.

93
00:13:05.803 --> 00:13:18.870
That is why albuterol is a bridge, used with calcium gluconate, insulin and dextrose, and definitive removal by diuretics, dialysis or resins.

94
00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:26.092
The dose is ten to twenty milligrams nebulized, far above the bronchodilator dose.

95
00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:29.793
Here is part one in brief.

96
00:13:29.793 --> 00:13:43.460
Acetylcysteine breaks disulfide bonds but is not recommended for lung disease; its testable use is acetaminophen overdose, and give a bronchodilator first.

97
00:13:43.460 --> 00:13:49.897
Beclomethasone is an inhaled steroid controller; rinse to prevent thrush.

98
00:13:49.897 --> 00:14:01.273
Albuterol and levalbuterol are rescue drugs with a fifteen minute onset; ipratropium blocks muscarinic tone and causes dry mouth.

99
00:14:01.273 --> 00:14:07.004
Long-acting beta agonists are controllers, never alone in asthma.

100
00:14:07.004 --> 00:14:15.910
Antileukotrienes are controllers too, and the biologics are add-ons, with anaphylaxis the worst risk.

101
00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:22.700
And albuterol lowers potassium by moving it into cells, a bridge, not a cure.
